{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "**Cherry Blossoms and Neon Lights: My Week in Japan**\n\nStepping off the plane in Tokyo felt like entering a different dimension. The air hummed with energy, a perfect blend of ancient tradition and futuristic innovation. My journey began in the bustling Shibuya Crossing, where thousands of people moved in synchronized chaos under a sea of neon signs. Yet, just a train ride away, I found silence in the Meiji Shrine, surrounded by towering cedar trees and the gentle rustle of wind through prayer flags.\n\nThe food was a revelation. From steaming bowls of tonkotsu ramen in tiny alleyway shops to delicate kaiseki dinners that looked too beautiful to eat, every meal was an experience. I still dream about the melt-in-your-mouth wagyu beef I tried in Osaka.\n\nTraveling to Kyoto shifted the pace entirely. Walking through the thousands of vermilion torii gates at Fushimi Inari Taisha at sunrise was magical. The light filtering through the gates created a path of gold and shadow. Later, I sat in a traditional tea house in Gion, watching a geisha hurry past in a flash of colorful silk.\n\nWhat struck me most was the omotenashi, the wholehearted hospitality. Everywhere I went, from convenience stores to ryokans, I was treated with genuine kindness and respect. The trains ran with impossible precision, and the streets were immaculate.\n\nJapan is a land of contrasts that somehow harmonize perfectly. One moment you are surrounded by cutting-edge technology, and the next, you are witnessing a centuries-old ritual. It's a place that stays with you long after you leave. If you haven't visited yet, put it at the top of your list. You won't regret it.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.06189906}, {"token": "Ch", "logprob": -0.041959435}, {"token": "erry", "logprob": -0.29454827}, {"token": " Bloss", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": "oms", "logprob": 0.0}, {"